  <header class="id-header">
    <p class="id-pillar">Questions · Behaviour</p>
    <h1>Do Ravens Use Tools?</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle">What the research shows — and how ravens compare to New Caledonian Crows</p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens show <strong>object manipulation and proto-tool behaviour</strong> but are not habitual tool users. New Caledonian Crows are the corvid tool-use specialists — they manufacture hooked tools in the wild. Common Ravens solve multi-step problems with insight but typically choose non-tool solutions when available. Studies by Taylor et al. and Heinrich suggest ravens have the cognitive capacity for tool use but do not reliably deploy it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>What "Tool Use" Means</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Animal behaviourists define tool use strictly: an animal uses an external object to change another object, substance, or organism, where the user holds or carries the tool and the tool is not attached to a substrate. By this definition:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Proto-tool use:</strong> using objects to displace other objects without holding or directing them precisely</li>
      <li><strong>Tool use:</strong> directing a held/carried object at a target to achieve an effect</li>
      <li><strong>Tool manufacture:</strong> modifying an object to make it more effective as a tool (the highest level)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>New Caledonian Crows operate at all three levels. Ravens have demonstrated proto-tool behaviour but not consistent, habitual tool use or manufacture in the wild.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>What Ravens Have Been Observed Doing</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Object manipulation:</strong> Ravens regularly pick up, carry, and manipulate objects in play contexts — without a food goal</li>
      <li><strong>Stone dropping:</strong> Anecdotal reports of ravens dropping objects onto intruders (documented by Gwinner 1965; described by Heinrich 2000), but not systematically verified as directed tool use</li>
      <li><strong>Displacement behaviours:</strong> Moving objects to get to a food reward underneath — proto-tool behaviour</li>
      <li><strong>Multi-step planning:</strong> Kabadayi & Osvath (2017) showed ravens could plan for a future tool-use task, selecting and saving a tool for later use. This demonstrates prospective cognition but was an experimental analogue, not wild behaviour</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Heinrich (2000) documents extensive object manipulation in ravens but notes that in food-getting contexts, ravens almost always solve problems without tools when possible — begging, stealing, or using their bill directly.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>New Caledonian Crow: The Corvid Benchmark</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> For context, New Caledonian Crows (<em>Corvus moneduloides</em>) represent the corvid extreme in tool use:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Manufacture hooked tools from pandanus leaf strips in the wild — the only non-human animal known to habitually make hooks</li>
      <li>Use metal wire to make hooks in lab settings (Weir et al. 2002)</li>
      <li>Use tools in sequential multi-step tasks (tool A to get tool B to get food)</li>
      <li>Tool use is widespread across the population and appears to be culturally transmitted</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Taylor et al. (2010) compared problem-solving strategies in NCC and found that they approach physical causation problems differently from other corvids — with more systematic causal reasoning about object relationships.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>The Kabadayi & Osvath (2017) Planning Study</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> This study (Science, 2017) tested whether ravens could plan for future tool-use tasks — a capacity thought to require "mental time travel" (thinking about a future need now):</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Ravens were shown a box that required a specific token to open (the "tool")</li>
      <li>Tokens were offered at a different time from when the box was accessible</li>
      <li>Ravens selected and saved the correct token for the future task — above chance, and despite competing temptations (immediate food rewards)</li>
      <li>Performance was comparable to great apes in equivalent paradigms</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Importantly: this tests prospective cognition and planning, not tool manufacture or habitual wild tool use. It shows the cognitive <em>capacity</em> without implying ravens deploy tools in nature.<sup><a href="#src-4">4</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Why Ravens May Not Use Tools Habitually</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Researchers offer several explanations:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Ecological niche:</strong> Ravens are generalist scavengers and predators — their beak is powerful enough to access most food without tools; NCC extract grubs from narrow holes, creating a specific selective pressure for tools</li>
      <li><strong>Social solutions:</strong> Ravens often solve food-access problems socially (recruitment, theft, coalition) rather than mechanically</li>
      <li><strong>Opportunity costs:</strong> Tool use is energetically costly if non-tool solutions exist; ravens appear to default to the simpler option</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Intelligence and tool use are not the same thing. Ravens show high-level cognition in many domains; tool use is simply not the domain in which their cognition is expressed most clearly.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

